``` Introduction

Arrays are created with `[]`. Backed by `Ore::Array`, a thin wrapper around a real Ruby array kept in `.values`. Elements are reachable by subscript or by dot notation -- both just index into the same underlying array.
```
raft_supplies := ['water', 'rope', 'tarp']
@puts "by dot notation: `raft_supplies.0`"    # by dot notation: water
@puts "by subscript: `raft_supplies[0]`"      # by subscript: water


``` push and pop

`push` adds to the end, `pop` removes from the end and returns what it removed.
```
raft_supplies.push('flare')
@puts "after push: `raft_supplies`"          # after push: [water, rope, tarp, flare]
@puts "popped: `raft_supplies.pop()`"        # popped: flare
@puts "after pop: `raft_supplies`"           # after pop: [water, rope, tarp]


``` length, first, last, reverse

```
@puts "length: `raft_supplies.length()`"      # length: 3
@puts "first 2: `raft_supplies.first(2)`"     # first 2: [water, rope]
@puts "last 2: `raft_supplies.last(2)`"       # last 2: [rope, tarp]
@puts "reversed: `raft_supplies.reverse()`"   # reversed: [tarp, rope, water]


``` include? and empty?

```
@puts "has rope? `raft_supplies.include?('rope')`"   # has rope? true
@puts "raft_supplies empty? `raft_supplies.empty?()`"  # raft_supplies empty? false
@puts "[] empty? `[].empty?()`"                       # [] empty? true


``` map and filter

Both take a function and run it against every element -- `map` collects each return value, `filter` keeps only elements the function returns truthy for.
```
survivors := ['Jack', 'Kate', 'Sawyer', 'Hurley']

shouted := survivors.map({ name;
	name.upcase()
})
@puts "shouted: `shouted`"   # shouted: [JACK, KATE, SAWYER, HURLEY]

long_names := survivors.filter({ name;
	name.length > 4
})
@puts "names longer than 4 letters: `long_names`"   # names longer than 4 letters: [Sawyer, Hurley]